1788529082780389	vessel-dev	itc:v3:AWLJkn-SeSTkkkQAAAAAQdav9XquqoAAAAEyYA		item.create	bn-3dzi	{"description":"# Context\n\nvessel spawn --cwd DIR correctly applies the directory to the child process, but the directory is not retained in AgentInfo. Consequently vessel list --format json cannot report where a long-lived agent was launched. Labels can describe a logical Bone or maw workspace, but they are user-supplied grouping metadata and are not an authoritative record of the effective launch directory.\n\nThe omission makes wrong-workspace mistakes and stale agents harder to audit after the original spawn command is gone.\n\n# Expected behavior\n\nWhen --cwd is supplied, Vessel should retain its effective value as agent metadata and expose it through machine-readable list output. The protocol change should remain backward compatible with peers or fixtures that do not include the field.\n\n# Acceptance criteria\n\n- AgentInfo carries an optional working-directory field with backward-compatible serde behavior.\n- A successful spawn with --cwd records a documented representation of the effective directory, preferably an absolute normalized path.\n- vessel list --format json includes cwd for running and retained exited agents spawned with --cwd.\n- Existing behavior remains compatible when --cwd is omitted.\n- Tests cover a temporary directory, a path containing spaces, JSON readback, exited-agent readback, and protocol deserialization without cwd.\n- CLI and protocol documentation explain whether cwd is the requested path or the resolved effective path.\n\n# Scope\n\nThis is observability for existing spawn behavior. It does not require Vessel to understand Bones, maw workspaces, Git commits, or scheduler job semantics.","kind":"bug","labels":["observability","metadata","cli"],"size":"s","title":"spawn --cwd is omitted from agent metadata and list output"}	blake3:prHWSPrAFFfvWOhL_jUEvSXpcnSLU_K6UH4ntbQzFLA
